<p>An unidentified assailant detonated an explosive device beside a traffic police patrol vehicle near Moscow’s Savyolovsky railway station shortly after midnight on Tuesday. The blast killed a police officer and the attacker, and it injured two other officers, Russian officials said. Officials have not publicly identified the attacker or offered a motive. Reporting noted the attack fell on the anniversary of Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine, a date that has taken on heightened symbolic weight during the war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/LtDNfrLNrzkuOIPbkeCdpKaXzGs_azjQNftNT8G2UZ_Fjbzwcymw5RZr9Idq8lTEQdJRnn4nef-6t7N7yuZUopd9Omujf17rtvj6QnrnJCTDbdp3hEcwzow3b6TbZGwSp5J2mb36kEmMDOhOI9ePpU6Znd2fkhd4RRiYbz1HSg' alt='Emergency response at the scene near Moscow’s Savyolovsky station after an explosion, according to ABC News/AP wire coverage.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Scene and response: Authorities cordoned off the area around the station while investigators and bomb technicians examined the scene. The two wounded officers were taken to hospital.</li><li>Timing detail: Russian officials said the blast went off minutes after midnight—around 12:05am local time—when the attacker approached the patrol vehicle.</li><li>What investigators said they established: Police said a close inspection of the scene and a review of external surveillance footage showed the perpetrator died at the site.</li><li>Ongoing probe: Moscow police said they were working with other security forces to establish the full circumstances of the explosion.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Moscow Interior Ministry branch: Officials said an assailant approached a traffic police patrol vehicle at Savyolovsky Square and detonated an explosive device. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/attacker-detonates-explosive-device-moscow-killing-police-officer-130429919'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Russian police press service: Police said surveillance footage and a detailed 